From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: geramyloveless <gloveless@jqluv.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 19:30:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [HAL/AMDGPU] Bracket a submission's BAR stores on the macOS
 runtime

On the mac_linuxgpu HSA runtime a CPU store to a GPU that stopped
answering (powered down, reset, or gone from the Thunderbolt link) can
panic the Mac. With kernel arguments in CPU-visible VRAM a submission
stores into the BARs three ways: the kernarg copy, the HDP flush store
and its read back, and the doorbell. The runtime offers the HDP flush
registers and CPU access to the device coarse pool only to a process
that brackets those stores under its gate.

With the macOS adapter, libhsa looks up the runtime's optional
mac_hsa_bar_writes_enable, mac_hsa_bar_write_begin and
mac_hsa_bar_write_end. A physical device opts in before querying
HSA_AMD_AGENT_INFO_HDP_FLUSH and fails creation if the runtime refuses,
rather than silently keeping kernel arguments in host memory. The
writer travels with the kernarg ring's HDP publication, and the host
queue's submission lock, including the transfer transactions', opens one
bracket per submission: every kernarg write, HDP publication and
doorbell already happens under that lock. The ring's first-touch store
is bracketed too. Profile event records, which the CPU writes outside a
submission, stay in host memory.

Apple silicon maps a BAR as device memory, where every access must be
aligned to its size. Kernel-argument copies and fills go through
iree_amdgpu_memcpy/memset, which with the adapter store only aligned
bytes and 64-bit words (memcpy/memset elsewhere, unchanged), and the
driver is compiled with -mstrict-align so no store is merged into a
wider unaligned one.

When the device is gone for the process the bracket does not open; the
runtime has already replaced every BAR mapping with host memory, so the
submission's stores land there and its doorbell fails.

Builds without IREE_HAL_AMDGPU_MACOS_COARSE_HOST_ADAPTER are unchanged.
